{=============================================================================
   Internal laminar convection and curved ducts: Nusselt-number correlations
   CoolSolve Library model CSL-0091 (function library, kind = function)
   -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Ten EES FUNCTIONs returning the Nusselt number Nu of single-phase internal
   flow at low Reynolds number or in non-circular / curved ducts:

   - fully developed laminar pipe flow, constant wall temperature (Nu = 3.66)
     and constant wall heat flux (Nu = 48/11 = 4.354);
   - laminar thermal entry region of a circular pipe (Hausen, Sieder-Tate,
     Baehr-Stephan), average Nu over the pipe length L;
   - fully developed laminar flow in a rectangular duct of aspect ratio a_r
     (Shah-London, constant wall heat flux);
   - turbulent flow in a spiral (Morimoto-Hotta) and in helical coils
     (Mori-Nakayama, Schmidt, Xin-Ebadian).

   As in the source library, the wall heat-transfer coefficient follows from
   h = Nu*k/Dh and is left to the caller; the friction factor is not an input
   of these correlations. The demonstration program at the end calls every
   function twice: once (case A) with the input set of the ht doctest of the
   corresponding correlation, once (case B) with a second input set that
   exercises the other branch of the two-branch correlations. The ht values
   of both sets are tabulated in the README (verification table).

   Inputs : Re [-], Pr [-], L [m], Di [m], mu / mu_w [Pa-s], a_r [-],
            Dh [m], Rm [m], Dc [m]
   Outputs: Nu [-] (one FUNCTION per correlation)
   Units  : SI - dimensionless numbers, viscosities in Pa-s, lengths in m
   Source : ht (Caleb Bell), ht/conv_internal.py, version 1.2.0, commit 85e0ee6,
            MIT - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht ; correlations by the authors
            cited in each function block - see README.md
   Status : VERIFIED against the doctests and the values of ht 1.2.0
            (README.md, "Verification")
 =============================================================================}

FUNCTION Nu_laminar_T_const(dummy)
{NU_LAMINAR_T_CONST - Nusselt number of fully developed laminar flow in a
circular pipe at constant wall temperature. Theoretically derived and
reproduced experimentally.

  Nu = 3.66

dummy [-] is an unused dummy argument: ht's function takes no arguments,
while EES requires at least one argument in a FUNCTION statement; the value
is always 3.66 whatever dummy is.

Validity: fully thermally and hydraulically developed laminar flow, constant
wall temperature (as in ht).
Original: Green, D. and R. Perry, Perry's Chemical Engineers' Handbook, 8E,
  McGraw-Hill, 2007; Bergman, T. L., A. S. Lavine, F. P. Incropera and
  D. P. DeWitt, Introduction to Heat Transfer, 6E, Wiley, 2011; VDI Heat
  Atlas, 2E, Springer, 2010.
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_internal.py, laminar_T_const,
  MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  Nu_laminar_T_const = 3.66
END

FUNCTION Nu_laminar_Q_const(dummy)
{NU_LAMINAR_Q_CONST - Nusselt number of fully developed laminar flow in a
circular pipe at constant wall heat flux. Theoretically derived and
reproduced experimentally.

  Nu = 48/11 = 4.354

dummy [-] is an unused dummy argument: ht's function takes no arguments,
while EES requires at least one argument in a FUNCTION statement; the value
is always 48/11 whatever dummy is. Many sources round to 4.36, but the VDI
Heat Atlas does not (as in ht).

Validity: fully thermally and hydraulically developed laminar flow, constant
wall heat flux (as in ht).
Original: Green, D. and R. Perry, Perry's Chemical Engineers' Handbook, 8E,
  McGraw-Hill, 2007; Bergman, T. L., A. S. Lavine, F. P. Incropera and
  D. P. DeWitt, Introduction to Heat Transfer, 6E, Wiley, 2011; VDI Heat
  Atlas, 2E, Springer, 2010.
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_internal.py, laminar_Q_const,
  MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  Nu_laminar_Q_const = 48/11
END

FUNCTION Nu_laminar_entry_thermal_Hausen(Re, Pr, L, Di)
{NU_LAMINAR_ENTRY_THERMAL_HAUSEN - average Nusselt number of laminar flow in
a circular pipe in the thermal entry region, over the pipe length L [m] of a
pipe of inside diameter Di [m].

  Gz = Di/L*Re*Pr
  Nu = 3.66 + 0.0668*Gz/(1 + 0.04*Gz^(2/3))

Re [-] is the Reynolds number on Di, Pr [-] the Prandtl number.
Validity: as in ht (no explicit range); if Pr >> 1 (5 is a common
  requirement) the equation also applies to flows with a developing velocity
  profile; as L grows, Nu becomes the constant-temperature value 3.66.
Original: Hausen, H., "Darstellung des Warmeuberganges in Rohren durch
  verallgeminerte Potenzbeziehungen", Z. Ver. deutsch. Ing. Beih.
  Verfahrenstech. 4: 91-98, 1943; as shown in Kays, W. M., "Numerical
  Solutions for Laminar Flow Heat Transfer in Circular Tubes", 1953; cited by
  Bergman, T. L., A. S. Lavine, F. P. Incropera and D. P. DeWitt,
  Introduction to Heat Transfer, 6E, Wiley, 2011.
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_internal.py,
  laminar_entry_thermal_Hausen, MIT License, C. Bell -
  https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  Gz_Hausen = Di/L*Re*Pr
  Nu_laminar_entry_thermal_Hausen = 3.66 + 0.0668*Gz_Hausen/(1 + 0.04*Gz_Hausen^(2/3))
END

FUNCTION Nu_laminar_entry_Seider_Tate(Re, Pr, L, Di, mu, mu_w)
{NU_LAMINAR_ENTRY_SIEDER_TATE - average Nusselt number of laminar flow in a
circular pipe in the thermal entry region, over the pipe length L [m] of a
pipe of inside diameter Di [m], with the viscosity correction for the wall
temperature.

  Nu = 1.86*(Di/L*Re*Pr)^(1/3)*(mu/mu_w)^0.14

Re [-] is the Reynolds number on Di, Pr [-] the Prandtl number, mu [Pa-s]
the bulk viscosity and mu_w [Pa-s] the viscosity at the wall temperature.
Passing mu = mu_w gives the form without the correction (both calls are
exercised in the demonstration program).
Validity: Reynolds number should be less than 10000 (as in ht); Prandtl
  number between that of air and that of liquid metals, 0.7 < Pr < 16700 (as
  in ht); outside the boundaries the equation gives very false results.
Original: Sieder, E. N. and G. E. Tate, "Heat Transfer and Pressure Drop of
  Liquids in Tubes", Industrial & Engineering Chemistry 28(12): 1429-1435,
  1936; also shown in Serth, R. W., Process Heat Transfer: Principles,
  Applications and Rules of Thumb, 2E, Academic Press, 2014.
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_internal.py,
  laminar_entry_Seider_Tate, MIT License, C. Bell -
  https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  Nu_laminar_entry_Seider_Tate = 1.86*(Di/L*Re*Pr)^(1/3)*(mu/mu_w)^0.14
END

FUNCTION Nu_laminar_entry_Baehr_Stephan(Re, Pr, L, Di)
{NU_LAMINAR_ENTRY_BAEHR_STEPHAN - average Nusselt number of laminar flow in
a circular pipe in the thermal and velocity entry region, over the pipe
length L [m] of a pipe of inside diameter Di [m].

  Gz = Di/L*Re*Pr
  Nu = (3.657/tanh(2.264*Gz^(-1/3) + 1.7*Gz^(-2/3))
        + 0.0499*Gz*tanh(Gz^(-1)))/tanh(2.432*Pr^(1/6)*Gz^(-1/6))

Re [-] is the Reynolds number on Di, Pr [-] the Prandtl number.
Validity: as in ht (no explicit range); as L grows, Nu becomes the
  constant-temperature value 3.66.
Original: Baehr, H. D. and K. Stephan, Heat and Mass Transfer, Springer,
  2013; as shown in Bergman, T. L., A. S. Lavine, F. P. Incropera and
  D. P. DeWitt, Introduction to Heat Transfer, 6E, Wiley, 2011.
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_internal.py,
  laminar_entry_Baehr_Stephan, MIT License, C. Bell -
  https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  Gz_BS = Di/L*Re*Pr
  Nu_laminar_entry_Baehr_Stephan = (3.657/tanh(2.264*Gz_BS^(-1/3) + 1.7*Gz_BS^(-2/3)) + 0.0499*Gz_BS*tanh(Gz_BS^(-1)))/tanh(2.432*Pr^(1/6)*Gz_BS^(-1/6))
END

FUNCTION Nu_laminar_rectangular_Shan_London(a_r)
{NU_LAMINAR_RECTANGULAR_SHAN_LONDON - Nusselt number of fully developed
laminar flow in a rectangular duct of aspect ratio a_r [-], from 0 to 1,
derived assuming a constant wall heat flux from all sides. Theoretically
derived and reproduced experimentally.

  Nu = 8.235*(1 - 2.0421*a_r + 3.0853*a_r^2 - 2.4765*a_r^3
              + 1.0578*a_r^4 - 0.1861*a_r^5)

Validity: as in ht (no explicit range); at an aspect ratio of 1 (square
  channel) Nu converges to 3.610224.
Original: Shah, R. K. and A. L. London, Supplement 1: Laminar Flow Forced
  Convection in Ducts: A Source Book for Compact Heat Exchanger Analytical
  Data, Academic Press, 1978; checked against Shah, R. K. and A. L. London,
  "Laminar Flow Forced Convection Heat Transfer and Flow Friction in Straight
  and Curved Ducts - A Summary of Analytical Solutions", Stanford Univ.,
  1971.
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_internal.py,
  Nu_laminar_rectangular_Shan_London, MIT License, C. Bell -
  https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  Nu_laminar_rectangular_Shan_London = 8.235*(1 - 2.0421*a_r + 3.0853*a_r^2 - 2.4765*a_r^3 + 1.0578*a_r^4 - 0.1861*a_r^5)
END

FUNCTION Nu_Morimoto_Hotta(Re, Pr, Dh, Rm)
{NU_MORIMOTO_HOTTA - Nusselt number of turbulent flow inside a spiral heat
exchanger of average spiral radius Rm [m] and average hydraulic diameter
Dh [m] (Dh = 2*H*S/(H+S) for a channel of height H and spacing S;
Rm = (R_min + R_max)/2).

  Nu = 0.0239*(1 + 5.54*Dh/Rm)*Re^0.806*Pr^0.268

Re [-] is the Reynolds number with bulk properties (on Dh), Pr [-] the
Prandtl number with bulk properties; Nu is with respect to Dh.
Validity: as in ht (no explicit range).
Original: Morimoto, E. and K. Hotta, "Study of Geometric Structure and Heat
  Transfer Characteristics of Spiral Plate Heat Exchanger", Transactions of
  the Japan Society of Mechanical Engineers Series B 52(474): 926-933, 1986
  (in Japanese); also shown in Bidabadi, M., A. Sadaghiani and A. Azad,
  "Spiral heat exchanger optimization using genetic algorithm", Int. J.
  Science and Technology 20(5): 1445-1454, 2013, and Turgut, O. E. and
  M. T. Coban, "Thermal Design of Spiral Heat Exchangers and Heat Pipes
  through Global Best Algorithm", Heat and Mass Transfer, 2016.
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_internal.py, Morimoto_Hotta,
  MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  Nu_Morimoto_Hotta = 0.0239*(1 + 5.54*Dh/Rm)*Re^0.806*Pr^0.268
END

FUNCTION Nu_helical_Mori_Nakayama(Re, Pr, Di, Dc)
{NU_HELICAL_MORI_NAKAYAMA - Nusselt number of turbulent flow inside a curved
pipe such as a helical coil, with the method of Mori and Nakayama.

  For Pr < 1:
  Nu = Pr/(26.2*(Pr^(2/3) - 0.074))*Re^0.8*(Di/Dc)^0.1
       * (1 + 0.098/(Re*(Di/Dc)^2)^0.2)
  For Pr >= 1:
  Nu = Pr^0.4/41*Re^(5/6)*(Di/Dc)^(1/12)
       * (1 + 0.061/(Re*(Di/Dc)^2.5)^(1/6))

Re [-] is the Reynolds number on Di, Pr [-] the Prandtl number with bulk
properties, Di [m] the inner diameter of the coil and Dc [m] the diameter of
the helix measured from the center of the tube on one side to the center of
the tube on the other side; Nu is with respect to Di.
Validity: applicable for Re*(Di/Dc)^2 > 0.1 (as in ht); at very low
  curvatures the predicted heat-transfer coefficient grows unbounded.
Original: Mori, Y. and W. Nakayama, "Study on Forced Convective Heat Transfer
  in Curved Pipes", Int. J. Heat and Mass Transfer 10(5): 681-695, 1967; also
  shown in El-Genk, M. S. and T. M. Schriener, "A Review and Correlations for
  Convection Heat Transfer and Pressure Losses in Toroidal and Helically
  Coiled Tubes", Heat Transfer Engineering, 2016, and Hardik, B. K.,
  P. K. Baburajan and S. V. Prabhu, "Local Heat Transfer Coefficient in
  Helical Coils with Single Phase Flow", Int. J. Heat and Mass Transfer 89:
  522-538, 2015.
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_internal.py,
  helical_turbulent_Nu_Mori_Nakayama, MIT License, C. Bell -
  https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  IF Pr < 1 THEN
    Nu_helical_Mori_Nakayama = Pr/(26.2*(Pr^(2/3) - 0.074))*Re^0.8*(Di/Dc)^0.1*(1 + 0.098/(Re*(Di/Dc)^2)^0.2)
  ELSE
    Nu_helical_Mori_Nakayama = Pr^0.4/41*Re^(5/6)*(Di/Dc)^(1/12)*(1 + 0.061/(Re*(Di/Dc)^2.5)^(1/6))
  ENDIF
END

FUNCTION Nu_helical_Schmidt(Re, Pr, Di, Dc)
{NU_HELICAL_SCHMIDT - Nusselt number of turbulent flow inside a curved pipe
such as a helical coil, with the method of Schmidt.

  For Re_crit < Re < 2.2E4:
  Nu = 0.023*(1 + 14.8*(1 + Di/Dc)*(Di/Dc)^(1/3))
       * Re^(0.8 - 0.22*(Di/Dc)^0.1)*Pr^(1/3)
  For 2.2E4 < Re < 1.5E5:
  Nu = 0.023*(1 + 3.6*(1 - Di/Dc)*(Di/Dc)^0.8)*Re^0.8*Pr^(1/3)

Re [-] is the Reynolds number on Di, Pr [-] the Prandtl number with bulk
properties, Di [m] the inner diameter of the coil and Dc [m] the diameter of
the helix measured from the center of the tube on one side to the center of
the tube on the other side; Nu is with respect to Di.
Validity: the two Reynolds-number ranges above (as in ht); for very low
  curvatures, reasonable results are returned by both cases.
Original: Schmidt, E. F., "Warmeubergang und Druckverlust in Rohrschlangen",
  Chemie Ingenieur Technik 39(13): 781-789, 1967; also shown in El-Genk, M. S.
  and T. M. Schriener, "A Review and Correlations for Convection Heat
  Transfer and Pressure Losses in Toroidal and Helically Coiled Tubes", Heat
  Transfer Engineering, 2016, Hardik, B. K., P. K. Baburajan and
  S. V. Prabhu, "Local Heat Transfer Coefficient in Helical Coils with
  Single Phase Flow", Int. J. Heat and Mass Transfer 89: 522-538, 2015, and
  Rohsenow, W., J. Hartnett and Y. Cho, Handbook of Heat Transfer, 3E,
  McGraw-Hill, 1998.
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_internal.py,
  helical_turbulent_Nu_Schmidt, MIT License, C. Bell -
  https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  IF Re <= 2.2E4 THEN
    Nu_helical_Schmidt = 0.023*(1 + 14.8*(1 + Di/Dc)*(Di/Dc)^(1/3))*Re^(0.8 - 0.22*(Di/Dc)^0.1)*Pr^(1/3)
  ELSE
    Nu_helical_Schmidt = 0.023*(1 + 3.6*(1 - Di/Dc)*(Di/Dc)^0.8)*Re^0.8*Pr^(1/3)
  ENDIF
END

FUNCTION Nu_helical_Xin_Ebadian(Re, Pr, Di, Dc)
{NU_HELICAL_XIN_EBADIAN - Nusselt number of turbulent flow inside a curved
pipe such as a helical coil, with the method of Xin and Ebadian.

  For Re_crit < Re < 1E5:
  Nu = 0.00619*Re^0.92*Pr^0.4*(1 + 3.455*Di/Dc)

Re [-] is the Reynolds number on Di, Pr [-] the Prandtl number with bulk
properties, Di [m] the inner diameter of the coil and Dc [m] the diameter of
the helix measured from the center of the tube on one side to the center of
the tube on the other side; Nu is with respect to Di.
Validity: the correlation was developed with data in the range
  0.7 < Pr < 5 and 0.0267 < Di/Dc < 0.0884 (as in ht); for very low
  curvatures, reasonable results are returned.
Original: Xin, R. C. and M. A. Ebadian, "The Effects of Prandtl Numbers on
  Local and Average Convective Heat Transfer Characteristics in Helical
  Pipes", Journal of Heat Transfer 119(3): 467-473, 1997; also shown in
  El-Genk, M. S. and T. M. Schriener, "A Review and Correlations for
  Convection Heat Transfer and Pressure Losses in Toroidal and Helically
  Coiled Tubes", Heat Transfer Engineering, 2016, and Hardik, B. K.,
  P. K. Baburajan and S. V. Prabhu, "Local Heat Transfer Coefficient in
  Helical Coils with Single Phase Flow", Int. J. Heat and Mass Transfer 89:
  522-538, 2015.
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_internal.py,
  helical_turbulent_Nu_Xin_Ebadian, MIT License, C. Bell -
  https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  Nu_helical_Xin_Ebadian = 0.00619*Re^0.92*Pr^0.4*(1 + 3.455*Di/Dc)
END

"Demonstration program - case A: inputs of the ht doctests"

"Nu_laminar_T_const and Nu_laminar_Q_const - constants, no inputs"
Nu_laminar_T_const_A = Nu_laminar_T_const(0)
Nu_laminar_Q_const_A = Nu_laminar_Q_const(0)

"Nu_laminar_entry_thermal_Hausen - Re = 1E5, Pr = 1.1, L = 5 m, Di = 0.5 m"
Re_Hausen_A = 1E5
Pr_Hausen_A = 1.1
L_Hausen_A = 5
Di_Hausen_A = 0.5
Nu_laminar_entry_thermal_Hausen_A = Nu_laminar_entry_thermal_Hausen(Re_Hausen_A, Pr_Hausen_A, L_Hausen_A, Di_Hausen_A)

"Nu_laminar_entry_Seider_Tate - Re = 1E5, Pr = 1.1, L = 5 m, Di = 0.5 m; without (mu = mu_w) and with the viscosity correction"
Re_ST_A = 1E5
Pr_ST_A = 1.1
L_ST_A = 5
Di_ST_A = 0.5
mu_ST_A = 1E-4
mu_w_ST_A = 1E-4
Nu_laminar_entry_Seider_Tate_A = Nu_laminar_entry_Seider_Tate(Re_ST_A, Pr_ST_A, L_ST_A, Di_ST_A, mu_ST_A, mu_w_ST_A)
Nu_laminar_entry_Seider_Tate_visc_A = Nu_laminar_entry_Seider_Tate(Re_ST_A, Pr_ST_A, L_ST_A, Di_ST_A, 8E-5, 1E-4)

"Nu_laminar_entry_Baehr_Stephan - Re = 1E5, Pr = 1.1, L = 5 m, Di = 0.5 m"
Re_BS_A = 1E5
Pr_BS_A = 1.1
L_BS_A = 5
Di_BS_A = 0.5
Nu_laminar_entry_Baehr_Stephan_A = Nu_laminar_entry_Baehr_Stephan(Re_BS_A, Pr_BS_A, L_BS_A, Di_BS_A)

"Nu_laminar_rectangular_Shan_London - aspect ratio 0.7"
a_r_A = 0.7
Nu_laminar_rectangular_Shan_London_A = Nu_laminar_rectangular_Shan_London(a_r_A)

"Nu_Morimoto_Hotta - Re = 1E5, Pr = 5.7, Dh = 0.05 m, Rm = 0.5 m"
Re_MH_A = 1E5
Pr_MH_A = 5.7
Dh_MH_A = 0.05
Rm_MH_A = 0.5
Nu_Morimoto_Hotta_A = Nu_Morimoto_Hotta(Re_MH_A, Pr_MH_A, Dh_MH_A, Rm_MH_A)

"Nu_helical_Mori_Nakayama - Re = 2E5, Pr = 0.7 (Pr < 1 branch), Di = 0.01 m, Dc = 0.2 m"
Re_MN_A = 2E5
Pr_MN_A = 0.7
Di_MN_A = 0.01
Dc_MN_A = 0.2
Nu_helical_Mori_Nakayama_A = Nu_helical_Mori_Nakayama(Re_MN_A, Pr_MN_A, Di_MN_A, Dc_MN_A)

"Nu_helical_Schmidt - Re = 2E5 (Re > 2.2E4 branch), Pr = 0.7, Di = 0.01 m, Dc = 0.2 m"
Re_Sch_A = 2E5
Pr_Sch_A = 0.7
Di_Sch_A = 0.01
Dc_Sch_A = 0.2
Nu_helical_Schmidt_A = Nu_helical_Schmidt(Re_Sch_A, Pr_Sch_A, Di_Sch_A, Dc_Sch_A)

"Nu_helical_Xin_Ebadian - Re = 2E5, Pr = 0.7, Di = 0.01 m, Dc = 0.2 m"
Re_XE_A = 2E5
Pr_XE_A = 0.7
Di_XE_A = 0.01
Dc_XE_A = 0.2
Nu_helical_Xin_Ebadian_A = Nu_helical_Xin_Ebadian(Re_XE_A, Pr_XE_A, Di_XE_A, Dc_XE_A)

"Demonstration program - case B: second input set (other branches)"

"Nu_laminar_T_const and Nu_laminar_Q_const - constants, no inputs"
Nu_laminar_T_const_B = Nu_laminar_T_const(0)
Nu_laminar_Q_const_B = Nu_laminar_Q_const(0)

"Nu_laminar_entry_thermal_Hausen - Re = 5000, Pr = 7, L = 2 m, Di = 0.02 m"
Re_Hausen_B = 5000
Pr_Hausen_B = 7
L_Hausen_B = 2
Di_Hausen_B = 0.02
Nu_laminar_entry_thermal_Hausen_B = Nu_laminar_entry_thermal_Hausen(Re_Hausen_B, Pr_Hausen_B, L_Hausen_B, Di_Hausen_B)

"Nu_laminar_entry_Seider_Tate - Re = 5000, Pr = 7, L = 2 m, Di = 0.02 m, with the viscosity correction"
Re_ST_B = 5000
Pr_ST_B = 7
L_ST_B = 2
Di_ST_B = 0.02
mu_ST_B = 4E-4
mu_w_ST_B = 6E-4
Nu_laminar_entry_Seider_Tate_B = Nu_laminar_entry_Seider_Tate(Re_ST_B, Pr_ST_B, L_ST_B, Di_ST_B, mu_ST_B, mu_w_ST_B)

"Nu_laminar_entry_Baehr_Stephan - Re = 5000, Pr = 7, L = 2 m, Di = 0.02 m"
Re_BS_B = 5000
Pr_BS_B = 7
L_BS_B = 2
Di_BS_B = 0.02
Nu_laminar_entry_Baehr_Stephan_B = Nu_laminar_entry_Baehr_Stephan(Re_BS_B, Pr_BS_B, L_BS_B, Di_BS_B)

"Nu_laminar_rectangular_Shan_London - aspect ratio 0.5"
a_r_B = 0.5
Nu_laminar_rectangular_Shan_London_B = Nu_laminar_rectangular_Shan_London(a_r_B)

"Nu_Morimoto_Hotta - Re = 5E4, Pr = 3, Dh = 0.03 m, Rm = 0.3 m"
Re_MH_B = 5E4
Pr_MH_B = 3
Dh_MH_B = 0.03
Rm_MH_B = 0.3
Nu_Morimoto_Hotta_B = Nu_Morimoto_Hotta(Re_MH_B, Pr_MH_B, Dh_MH_B, Rm_MH_B)

"Nu_helical_Mori_Nakayama - Re = 1E5, Pr = 5 (Pr >= 1 branch), Di = 0.02 m, Dc = 0.1 m"
Re_MN_B = 1E5
Pr_MN_B = 5
Di_MN_B = 0.02
Dc_MN_B = 0.1
Nu_helical_Mori_Nakayama_B = Nu_helical_Mori_Nakayama(Re_MN_B, Pr_MN_B, Di_MN_B, Dc_MN_B)

"Nu_helical_Schmidt - Re = 1E4 (Re <= 2.2E4 branch), Pr = 3, Di = 0.02 m, Dc = 0.1 m"
Re_Sch_B = 1E4
Pr_Sch_B = 3
Di_Sch_B = 0.02
Dc_Sch_B = 0.1
Nu_helical_Schmidt_B = Nu_helical_Schmidt(Re_Sch_B, Pr_Sch_B, Di_Sch_B, Dc_Sch_B)

"Nu_helical_Xin_Ebadian - Re = 5E4, Pr = 3, Di = 0.02 m, Dc = 0.1 m"
Re_XE_B = 5E4
Pr_XE_B = 3
Di_XE_B = 0.02
Dc_XE_B = 0.1
Nu_helical_Xin_Ebadian_B = Nu_helical_Xin_Ebadian(Re_XE_B, Pr_XE_B, Di_XE_B, Dc_XE_B)
